.TH GETGRENT 3
.SH NAME
getgrent, getgrgid, getgrnam, setgrent, endgrent \- get group file entry
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <grp.h>
.PP
.B struct group *getgrent(void);
.PP
.B struct group *getgrgid(int gid);
.PP
.B struct group *getgrnam(const char *name);
.PP
.B void setgrent(void);
.PP
.B void endgrent(void);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Getgrent,
.I getgrgid
and
.I getgrnam
each return pointers
to an object
with the following structure
containing the broken-out
fields of a line in the group file.
.RS
.PP
.nf
struct group {
    char  *gr_name;
    char  *gr_passwd;
    int    gr_gid;
    char **gr_mem;
};
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B Note:
v7's page pulled this in with a
.B .so
of the installed header.  It is written out here, because there is no installed
/usr/include on this system \- the one header tree is
share/besm6/include.  <grp.h> declares all five routines too; v7's declared the
struct alone, leaving the rest to K&R's implicit
.BR int .
.PP
The members of this structure are:
.TP
gr_name
The name of the group.
.br
.ns
.TP
gr_passwd
The encrypted password of the group.
.br
.ns
.TP
gr_gid
The numerical group-ID.
.br
.ns
.TP
gr_mem
Null-terminated vector
of pointers to the individual
member names.
.PP
.I Getgrent
simply reads the next
line while
.I getgrgid
and
.I getgrnam
search until a matching
.I gid
or
.I name
is found
(or until EOF is encountered).
Each routine picks up
where the others leave off
so successive calls may be used
to search the entire file.
.PP
A call to
.I setgrent
has the effect of rewinding
the group file
to allow
repeated searches.
.I Endgrent
may be called to
close the group file
when processing is complete.
.SH FILES
/etc/group
.br
share/besm6/lib/libc.a, lib/libc/stdio/getgrent.c, getgrnam.c, getgrgid.c
.SH "SEE ALSO"
getlogin(3), getpwent(3), group(5)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
A null pointer
(0) is returned on EOF or error.
.SH BUGS
All information
is contained in a static area
so it must be copied if it is
to be saved.
.B The struct, the strings and the member vector are all overwritten by the next
.BR call .
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
The line buffer is
.BR GRLINE ,
not BUFSIZ, for the reason
.IR getpwent (3)
gives: BUFSIZ is 3072 here and describes a disk block rather than a line of text.
.PP
One v7 bug is fixed rather than carried, and it is in the member list \- the one
field /etc/passwd has no counterpart to.  v7 sized the
.I gr_mem
vector at MAXGRP and then
.B filled it without checking,
so a group line with more members than that walked off the end of a file-scope
array into whatever bss followed it.  The walk stops with room for the terminator
now, and the tail is dropped.  A 512-byte line cannot hold a hundred names in any
case; the bound is the array's, not the format's.
